hey made any direct demands in  terms of what they would want for this  protest action to end in winnipeg  uh from my go ahead yeah i mean  i i'm not i haven't had any direct  contact with them so i'm not able to  answer that question so i'll turn it  over to you chief  and in terms of our uh  interaction with we're just there to  make sure that people are able to  demonstrate peacefully and that the  disruptions are minimized as much as  possible uh they haven't shared any  demands with us  okay thank you um  i guess mayor i'd be curious in your own  opinion in your feelings does a protest  like this do anything to you know impact  how you may view current public health  measures or vaccine mandates do you  think it will be effective in changing  some of the pre-existing stances on the  culvert 19 health and safety protocol  short answer is no  i think the  protesters are making their views clear  and they're entitled to do so  it's my hope and expectation that all  levels of government will guide  decisions around  vaccines and  other covid  public health measures based on the best  advice from health care professionals  that are grounded in science and and  fact and so  you know i think that that's where the  those decisions should be should be made  and i'll continue to support decisions  that are made on the basis of the advice  from health care professionals that  being said uh canadians  will have  and do have the ability and the right to  share their views on what they think of  those measures but it's my hope and  expectation that all levels of  government will continue to support  and be guided going forward based on the  the best advice from healthcare  professionals  brittany from global  good afternoon gentlemen thanks so much  for uh taking the time this afternoon  mayor bowman i just wanted to get your  thoughts on on what you've seen so far  from the protest and maybe chief smite  as well um you know the difference in  what you're seeing or are you seeing one  what we're seeing here in winnipeg  compared to what we're seeing in  protests in other cities and how you're  feeling about that so far  well i'm appreciative of the work that's  being undertaken by  our public service including the  winnipeg police service i'm appreciative  of the work  that's going on  with other levels of government  including the provincial government  you know  we'll have to see how  you know how things unfold  but at this stage  based on the information we have it's  been  peaceful and  and that's what we want to continue to  see  and we want to see the least impact on  the ability for our residents to go  about their daily lives and so  you know hopefully that will uh that's  that that will be the path forward  thank you and i guess um i don't know if  this is maybe for you or maybe for chief  smith there's been concerns in ottawa  that police and and the city there let  things get too far and are getting a bit  out of control what kind of contingency  plans are in place here if this  continues to go forward past just this  one day  is that for me  sure  i i guess first i would say that  every protest and every location is  unique in its own right  uh and it's  probably not fair to be comparing us to  ottawa at the moment  we have an open dialogue going with the  organizers that are here  on the ground locally  they have been  very open with us and and to date uh and  we're only into it  by six hours or so a very cooperative  with us so  um  you know i i don't think it's a fair  comparison looking at ottawa  anne from radio canada  hi  um my question is for advancement um i  know i'm sorry for mr bullman but as  well there could be as well for two  um  since you've met with the mayor of the  city of odawa uh what have you been  discussed what have you discussed about  and  was that  concerning  a plan to prevent what is happening  right now in ottawa  um so  i speak regularly with mayors across the  country  you know these kinds of protests are  happening in  in other locations as well  my primary reason for connecting with  with mayor watson was to just offer  support  and also to  just see how he was doing it's obviously  not a  uh not an ideal situation right now in  in ottawa  um you know  one of the things we just discussed is  you know the the fact that people have  the the lawful right to protest but it's  uh it's a challenge when uh those  uh those uh protests uh begin  uh in a in a lengthy way uh adversely  affecting local businesses and so we  just talked about that  again wanted to just offer my support to  him i know it's it's not an easy  situation right now and appreciative of  the work of the public service  in ottawa and the capital region  respective  agencies i know are doing their best  thank you and for now they have an  estimate of the number of people that  have participated in the manufa the  protest today and the people that will  come during the next day probably  i'm sorry were you asking for an  estimate of what's going on at the  moment an estimate of the number of  people you've said a number of trucks  but do you have an estimated number of  people  yeah we we estimate between 50 and 70  uh vehicles that are down on the site  right now i'm not sure what that equates  to in terms of people most of the people  are are in their vehicles from my  understanding  so it's been very manageable at the  moment the biggest preventative thing  that we have going for us is an ongoing  dialogue with the organizers so that  everybody understands  their rights and  and what the expectations are  thank you  sam from cbc did you have any final  questions  i do yes  uh this is for chief smythe um the  winnipeg city council put out a joint  statement condemning the use of um  racist symbols and comments during the  protests not only here in manitoba but  across  canada and i'm wondering if you'd like  to offer comment on the winnipeg police  services point of view on the use of  those images during these protests  yeah  i would share in  in that position uh i don't think  equating what's going on here with  symbols of oppression and nazism is  appropriate and and i share that  sentiment  okay thank you and the other question i  have i saw the police service tweeted um  that protesters got everybody to sign a  code of conduct um to participate in it  do you have any information on what's in  that code of conduct was that shared  with police  i i was aware that they had set up a  code of conduct amongst themselves i've  not seen it myself but  i was certainly informed of it so  um i i don't know what specifically is  in the code of conduct but again  my understanding is it's their  ability to demonstrate to us that  they're there uh peacefully  uh and just my other question um  i know it seems like a basic question  but what does it mean for protesters to  cooperate with police i like many people  may not have even participated in  protests before they might not  understand what that looks like so from  your point of view  what does cooperating  mean and look like  so typically when we become aware of any  kind of demonstration or protest  generally speaking we'll we'll reach out  to the organizers so that they  understand what you know their  constitutional rights are around  protesting but also an understanding of  how we can assist them in keeping safe  often protests take place  in public spaces and on public roadways  so we want to assist organizers so that  they can demonstrate in a way that  doesn't jeopardize their own safety or  the safety of other people so that's  first and foremost uh  generally speaking we reach out to offer  our assistance so that people  can  can protest as is their right  in a safe and peaceful manner and if  there's anything that we can do  to facilitate that we will  conversely we want to make sure that  they understand that you know they they  can't break the law per se they you know  we don't want to see anything escalate  that would become  unsafe or unruly  danielle from the winnipeg free press  thanks  so this one is for chief smythe and for  jay shaw so what are your biggest  concerns  operationally if the demonstration  does go for an extended period of time  from my perspective it would be just the  disruption for the  people and the businesses operating in  the area  we recognize that in a democratic  society we put up with some disruption  for protests but it would be  uh it would be hard for the people that  live and work in that area  and i would just echo what uh what chief  smith is saying in terms of the the  impacts to residents and  uh possibly some uh you know when we  look at critical infrastructure in terms  of access to  uh for emergency services we just want  everyone to stay safe so  i think then i do have a couple more i  was just  wanting to confirm whether the wps have  received any complaints related to  the demonstration uh so far or if there  have been any reports of altercations  between folks  we have not  james from the winnipeg sun  james is left uh devin from ctv  uh just one more for chief smith here  just i know you've said that you're not  really sure how long  they could be around protesting but um  if they are around for the weekend what  are the plans uh moving forward for  saturday and sunday  it would be more of the same we would  keep an ongoing dialogue with the  organizers to ensure uh that everyone  uh proceeds safely and  you know that we minimize the  disruptions  uh as much as we can  that's everything thank you  brittany from global  i'm good thank you very much  anne from radio canada  no question thank you  uh i'll just check one more time sam uh  from cbc are you good no i have one more  question please um this is for mayor  bowman uh you mentioned you spoke with  premier stephenson earlier today you  said uh about the need for calm i'm just  wondering if you can elaborate on that  why  why that came up  i i think there's just a recognition  that um  the uh  the issues that are being discussed  right now in some of these protests are  very emotional and  i think what is important for our  residents to know  is that government officials are are  doing their best to respect the the  rights of of our residents  both those protesting and  you know and as well as winnipeggers  themselves who  um you know may not share um  the the views of those protesting um and  so  you know primarily i i want to just  ensure the lines of communication are  are open uh hopefully they're not needed  for a very long time  we do talk regularly and we'll talk as  needed throughout the weekend and into  next week  but i  i convey to her our appreciation for the  work that her officials are doing right  now in and around the legislature as  well as the work that's being undertaken  by the the city of winnipeg  personnel including um you know both jay  shaw and and chief smythe and and all of  their uh team members  um i think you know in terms of um  you know some of the  images that we saw in our community last  week which um you know our council has  unanimously condemned  what we're seeing in in ottawa  in terms of the prolonged nature and  some of the  pretty significant disruptions to  to  you know the ability for the city and  its residents to go about their  businesses  we just want to ensure that there is  there is calm right now  that we recognize the the lawful ability  for peaceful protest but also that those  temporary disruptions shouldn't be  prolonged and so  we'll obviously just keep those lines of  communication open  as they have in in recent weeks since  she became premier but certainly with  just  what we're seeing in in other cities um  i wanted to ensure the lines of  communication we're open with the  premier and um she's obviously in in  agreement with that  thank you okay no problem  danielle from the free press did you  have any more questions no good thank  you uh and last one devin from ctv did  you have any further questions  no i'm good thank you  all right i believe in that conclusion i  still have a question oh sorry mark okay  we'll go to you  thank you uh i just wanted to ask like  there's a very prominent and massive  american flag next to a canadian one do  you know if any of the protesters have  come from the united states to winnipeg  for this  i i certainly can't speak to that i i  would presume based on what we've heard  there's a number of non-winnipeggers  that are um that are at the the protest  um that's their their uh they have the  ability people have the the right to  travel and i can't explain why there's a  such a large american flag there but  again um you know people have the  ability to uh  to uh to have a flag of that nature and  i but i couldn't i couldn't speak to  where people's residency or citizenship  is  chief smythe any chance you would know  no i'm not aware of the origins of any  of the protesters  thank you  all right now i believe that concludes  our press conference thank you thank you  everyone uh thank you chief thank you  jay shaw uh have a great weekend  everybody merci maguitch
 

